  "answer_text": "In the world of paleontology and archaeology, fossilized poop is a scientific goldmine. Officially known as a **coprolite** (from the Greek words *kopros*, meaning dung, and *lithos*, meaning stone), these ancient droppings provide some of the most intimate and detailed clues about life on Earth millions of years ago.\n\n### What is a Coprolite?\nCoprolites are classified as **trace fossils**\u2014fossils that record the behavior or activity of an organism rather than its actual body. \n\nWhen an animal pooped millions of years ago, the organic material was occasionally covered by sediment before it could decompose or be eaten. Over time, through a process called mineralization, minerals (most commonly calcium phosphate, silicates, or calcium carbonate) slowly replaced the organic matter. \n\n**Is it smelly?** No! Because they have been completely turned to stone, coprolites do not smell, and they are completely safe to handle.\n\n---\n\n### Why Scientists Love Fossilized Poop\nWhile dinosaur bones can tell us what an animal looked like, coprolites tell us **how they lived**. They are essentially biological time capsules. By slicing coprolites into thin sections or scanning them with CT technology, scientists can discover:\n* **Diet:** Coprolites often contain undigested bits of food, such as fish scales, bone fragments, seeds, pollen, and leaves. For example, fossilized T. rex poop has been found containing shattered bones, proving how powerful its bite was, while other dinosaur dung showed they ate grass, rewriting our understanding of when grass first evolved.\n* **Parasites & Disease:** Human and animal coprolites frequently contain fossilized parasite eggs (like roundworms or tapeworms), giving us a direct look at prehistoric health and hygiene.\n* **Ecosystems:** They help map out ancient food webs\u2014revealing exactly who was eating whom in a given environment.\n\n---\n\n### Famous Coprolites\n\n#### 1. The Lloyds Bank Coprolite (The \"Viking Turd\")\nThis is the most famous piece of human poop (often called *paleofeces*) in history. Discovered in 1972 under what is now a Lloyds Bank branch in York, England, this massive specimen is **8 inches long and 2 inches wide**. It was left behind by a Viking in the 9th century AD. \n* **What it revealed:** The Viking had a diet consisting mostly of meat and bread. However, he was also riddled with parasitic whipworms and maw-worms. \n* Today, it is on display at the Jorvik Viking Centre in York and is considered by some archaeologists to be as \"precious as the crown jewels\".\n\n#### 2. \"Barnum\" (The T. Rex Coprolite)\n\"Barnum\" is the largest fossilized poop from a carnivorous animal ever discovered. Found in South Dakota, USA, this massive coprolite weighs **20.5 pounds (9.28 kg)** and is believed to have been left behind by a *Tyrannosaurus rex*. It resides in the Poozeum, a museum dedicated entirely to fossilized feces.\n\n---\n\n### How to Tell a Coprolite Apart from a Regular Rock\nIt can be hard to distinguish a coprolite from an ordinary, lumpy rock, but geologists look for a few distinct clues:\n1. **The Shape:** Many coprolites have distinct shapes, like spirals (common in ancient sharks and fish) or segmented log-like shapes.\n2. **Inclusions:** If you look closely or cut it open, a coprolite will often have tiny fragments of bones, shells, scales, or plant material embedded inside.\n3. **Chemical Composition:** Coprolites are very rich in calcium phosphate (a major component of both bone and feces). \n4. **The \"Lick Test\":** An old (and slightly gross) geologist trick to identify fossil bone or coprolite is to lightly touch it to your tongue. Because fossilized bone and feces are highly porous, they will slightly stick to your tongue, whereas a normal rock will not. \n\nFrom ancient sharks and dinosaurs to Viking warriors, coprolites prove that even the most disposable parts of history can become priceless scientific treasures!",
